\v 1 After¦96575 this, \add @Paul\add* left¦96578 Athens¦96584 and travelled to¦96586 Corinth¦96587.
\v 2 There he came¦96598 across a¦96590 Jew named¦96592 Aquila¦96593 who had been born in Pontus¦96594 but who had recently¦96597 moved from¦96600 Italy¦96602 along with his¦96606 wife¦96605 Priscilla¦96604 because¦96607 \add Emperor\add* Claudius¦96611 had¦96610 ordered all¦96614 Jews¦96616 to¦96613 leave¦96613 Rome¦96620. So \add @Paul¦96627\add* approached¦96627 them¦96628,
\v 3 and¦96632 since¦96633 they worked in the¦96634 same¦96635 trade¦96635, he¦96637 ended up staying¦96637 with¦96638 them¦96640 and¦96642 they¦96644 worked¦96644 together in \add &their¦96648\add* craft¦96650 of tent-making.
\v 4 On¦96661 every¦96662 Rest¦96663 Day¦96663, \add @Paul¦96653\add* debated in¦96655 the¦96657 \add ≈Jewish¦96659 meeting¦96659 hall¦96659\add*, and was¦96673 persuading¦96673 both¦96674 Jews¦96678 and¦96680 Greeks¦96681.
\p
\v 5 However when¦96682 Silas¦96692 and¦96693 Timothy¦96695 arrived¦96685 from¦96686 Macedonia¦96688, Paul¦96701 was¦96696 able to devote his full time to the¦96697 message¦96698—demonstrating to¦96703 the¦96703 Jews¦96704 that \nd Yeshua¦96709\nd* is¦96705 the¦96706 \add promised\add* \nd messiah¦96707\nd*.
\v 6 But when they opposed¦96717 \add the message\add* and¦96722 made false accusations, he \add publicly\add* brushed \add the dust\add* off¦96724 his¦96727 clothes¦96728\f + \fr 18:6 \ft In the culture, this was a demonstration that you're not responsible for their decision(s).\f* and said¦96730, “Your¦96735 future deaths are¦96735 your¦96739 own problem. I'm¦96741 innocent \add of your guilt.\add* From¦96742 now¦96745 on, I'll¦96749 going¦96749 to¦96746 the¦96747 non-Jews.”
\v 7 \add ≈So¦96751\add* \add @Paul\add* left¦96752 there¦96756, and went¦96757 to¦96759 stay at the¦96762 house¦96762 of¦96763 a man named¦96764 Titos Justus who worshipped¦96769 \nd God¦96771\nd* and whose house¦96774 was¦96775 next-door to¦96777 the¦96777 \add ≈meeting¦96778 hall¦96778\add*.
\v 8 Krispo the¦96782 \add ≈meeting¦96783 hall¦96783\add* leader¦96783 and all¦96792 his¦96795 household¦96794 \add ≈decided to follow\add* the¦96787 \nd master¦96789\nd*, and¦96796 many¦96797 of¦96798 the¦96798 Corinthians¦96799 believed¦96785 after they heard¦96800 \add the message\add* and¦96802 were¦96803 immersed¦96803.
\p
\v 9 Then¦96816 the¦96817 \nd master¦96818\nd* spoke¦96815 to¦96827 Paul¦96827 one night¦96820 in a¦96822 vision¦96822, “Don't be¦96831 afraid¦96831 and¦96834 stay silent¦96836, but keep¦96836 speaking¦96833 out
\v 10 because¦96838 I¦96839 am¦96840 with¦96841 you¦96842 and¦96843 no¦96844 one¦96844 can mistreat¦96848 you¦96849 here because¦96850 there¦96852 are¦96852 many¦96854 of my people¦96851 in¦96855 this¦96858 city¦96857.”
\v 11 \add ≈So¦96861\add* \add @Paul¦96860\add* stayed on for eighteen months¦96869, teaching¦96871 \nd God's¦96878\nd* message¦96876 to the¦96877 people there.
\p
\v 12 Then at the time when Gallio¦96879 was¦96884 the proconsul¦96883 of¦96886 Achaia¦96886, the¦96889 Jews¦96890 united against Paul¦96898 and¦96900 brought¦96904 a case to¦96907 the¦96908 tribunal¦96909 against him
\v 13 saying¦96912, “This¦96919 man¦96919 is¦96918 going against the¦96915 law¦96916 by persuading¦96918 people¦96921 to¦96922 be¦96922 worshipping¦96922 \add &his¦96923\add* \nd God¦96924\nd*.”
