\v 3 After sighting Cyprus¦99313 and¦99314 then leaving¦99315 it¦99317 behind on¦99318 our left¦99318, we¦99319 sailed¦99319 on to¦99320 Syria¦99321 and¦99322 docked there¦99327 at¦99325 the port of Tyre¦99326 where the¦99330 ship¦99331 had cargo¦99335 to be¦99332 unloaded¦99333.
\v 4 We searched¦99337 for the¦99339 believers there¦99343 and then stayed¦99341 on¦99341 with them for seven¦99345 days¦99344. They were¦99349 inspired by¦99350 the¦99351 \nd spirit¦99352\nd* to¦99348 tell¦99349 Paul¦99348 not¦99354 to¦99355 go to¦99357 Yerushalem¦99359.
\v 5 Once our time¦99367 was up there and¦99361 everyone knew that we¦99369 were¦99369 leaving, even the wives¦99374 and¦99375 children¦99376 accompanied¦99370 us¦99371 out of the¦99379 city¦99380 to the port. We all¦99372 knelt¦99382 on¦99386 the¦99387 beach¦99388 and prayed¦99390
\v 6 before we¦99393 continued on and¦99396 boarded \add &our¦99401\add* ship¦99402 and¦99404 they went¦99398 back to¦99406 their¦99407 own¦99408 homes¦99408.
\p
\v 7 \add ≈So¦99410\add* we¦99409 sailed¦99412 from¦99414 Tyre¦99415 down to¦99418 Ptolemais¦99419 where we were able to greet¦99421 the¦99422 \add ≈brothers¦99423 and¦99423 sisters¦99423\add* and stay¦99425 overnight with¦99428 them¦99429.
\v 8 The¦99430 next¦99432 day¦99430 we¦99439 continued on to¦99440 Caesarea¦99441 and¦99442 ended up in¦99443 Philip's¦99447 house¦99446. He was¦99451 one of the¦99448 \add original\add* seven¦99454 \add men who had been given the responsibility of distributing aid\add*—a preacher¦99449 of¦99452 the¦99453 good¦99449 message¦99449, and we¦99455 stayed¦99455 with¦99456 him¦99457.
\v 9 \add @Philip\add* had four¦99464 unmarried daughters¦99462 \add still at home\add*, and they shared messages from God.
\v 10 Several days¦99471 later a¦99474 man named¦99479 Agabus¦99480 came¦99473 from¦99475 Yudea¦99477. He too shared messages from God
\v 11 and¦99481 when he arrived¦99482, he picked¦99488 up¦99488 Paul's¦99492 belt and tied¦99493 it around his own feet¦99499 and¦99501 hands¦99504, telling¦99506 them that the¦99511 holy¦99512 \nd spirit¦99510\nd* had told¦99508 him that this¦99519 is¦99508 how the¦99513 owner of¦99515 the¦99517 belt¦99518 would be treated by the¦99525 Jewish leaders in¦99523 Yerushalem¦99524 before they¦99528 would¦99528 then hand¦99528 him over¦99528 to¦99531 the non-Jews to be dealt with.
\p
\v 12 When¦99535 we¦99537 heard¦99537 this described, both¦99542 we¦99541 and¦99543 the¦99545 locals¦99545 begged¦99539 \add @Paul¦99552\add* not¦99549 to¦99551 go¦99551 to¦99553 Yerushalem¦99554
\v 13 but \add *he\add* answered¦99556, “Oh come on, what's¦99566 all¦99567 this weeping¦99568 and¦99569 carrying on that's breaking¦99571 my¦99572 heart¦99574? Actually I'm¦99588 quite ready¦99587 not¦99577 only¦99578 to¦99579 be¦99579 tied¦99579 up, but¦99581 even to¦99583 be killed in¦99585 Yerushalem¦99586 as a testimony to the¦99594 \nd master¦99595 Yeshua¦99596\nd*.”
\p
\v 14 When¦99600 we saw that we couldn't \add ≈change his mind\add*, we didn't¦99598 say¦99604 anything else, figuring out that whatever the¦99609 \nd master¦99608\nd* wills¦99610 for the¦99607 situation would happen¦99614,
\rem /s1 Paul Arrives at Jerusalem
\v 15 and¦99617 so after¦99616 these¦99621 several days¦99620, we¦99627 had¦99622 gotten everything ready and started off for Yerushalem¦99631.
\v 16 Some of¦99635 the¦99635 believers from¦99637 Caesarea¦99639 accompanied us¦99641 and led us¦99642 to¦99647 the home of a man named Mnason¦99647 to stay—he was an older believer from¦99649 Cyprus¦99649.
\s1 Paul visits Yacob and makes a vow
\rem /s1 Paul's Arrival at Jerusalem; Paul Visits James at Jerusalem
\p
\v 17 When¦99653 we arrived at¦99655 Yerushalem¦99656, the¦99661 \add ≈brothers¦99662 and¦99662 sisters¦99662\add* there happily welcomed¦99658 us¦99660,
\v 18 and¦99666 the¦99663 next day¦99663, we accompanied Paul¦99672 to¦99675 go and see Yacob¦99676\f + \fr 21:18 \ft Yacob is commonly (but wrongly) called James in most older English Bibles.\f* and¦99678 the¦99680 elders¦99681 of the assembly there.
\v 19 After greeting¦99685 them¦99687, \add @Paul¦99689\add* then¦99683 described all the¦99700 things that \nd God¦99701\nd* had done¦99699 among¦99702 the¦99703 non-Jews as he had served¦99707 among them,
\v 20 and¦99710 when they'd¦99709 heard¦99711 all¦99735 that¦99732 they praised¦99712 \nd God¦99715\nd* and¦99719 told¦99718 \add @Paul\add*, “Brother¦99722, you've noticed how¦99723 many¦99723 thousands¦99724 of Jews¦99729 have¦99733 believed¦99733 \add in the messiah\add* but¦99734 they¦99740 \add still\add* strive to observe¦99721 \add &Mosheh's¦99738\add* \add #laws¦99739\add*.
\v 21 But¦99742 they've¦99741 heard reports that¦99745 you've¦99747 been¦99747 teaching¦99747 the Jews that live outside our country that they don't have to obey what Mosheh¦99749 \add wrote\add*—telling¦99758 them¦99762 that they don't¦99760 have to¦99761 have their¦99763 children¦99764 circumcised¦99761 and that they don't have to¦99772 follow \add &our¦99768\add* \add Jewish\add* customs¦99769.
\v 22 What¦99773 will happen \add now\add*, because \add the leaders here\add* will certainly¦99776 hear¦99781 that¦99783 you¦99784 have¦99784 arrived¦99784?
\v 23 So¦99786 here's what¦99788 we¦99790 suggest that¦99785 you¦99789 do: Four¦99794 of our men¦99793 are¦99791 making a¦99795 \add public\add* vow¦99795.
\v 24 \add ^Why don't\add* you join these¦99800 four and join them in the \add ≈purification¦99802\add* ceremony \add at the temple\add* and¦99806 pay their expenses in¦99812 getting your \add #heads¦99817\add* shaved¦99813. Then¦99818 everyone¦99821 will¦99819 be¦99819 able to see¦99819 that¦99822 it's false what they've¦99825 heard about¦99826 you¦99828, because you¦99835 \add clearly\add* \add still\add* comply with \add &Mosheh's¦99839\add* instructions¦99840.
\v 25 And as for the¦99844 believers¦99845 who are non-Jews, we've¦99852 informed them¦99867 of our recommendation that they should avoid anything that's been offered to¦99866 an idol¦99870, avoid consuming blood¦99873 and¦99874 meat from strangled¦99875 animals¦99875 and birds, and avoid sexual¦99877 immorality¦99877.”
\rem /s1 Paul Is Arrested
\p
\v 26 So the¦99879 next day¦99887 Paul¦99880 took¦99881 the¦99882 \add four\add* men¦99883 for the¦99884 purification¦99890 ceremony in¦99893 the¦99894 temple¦99895 and there they announced¦99896 the date when their vows \add ≈would be¦99906 fulfilled¦99898\add* and their¦99910 sacrifices¦99912 made.
\s1 Paul is captured inside the temple
\rem /s1 Paul Is Arrested in the Temple; Paul's work in Jerusalem
\p
\v 27 When¦99913 the¦99917 seven¦99919 days¦99921 \add of the vow\add* were¦99916 almost over, some Jews¦99930 from¦99927 \add the province of\add* Asia¦99929 \add Minor\add* noticed¦99932 \add @Paul¦99933\add* in¦99934 the¦99935 temple¦99936. They stirred up the¦99945 crowd¦99946 and¦99947 grabbed hold of him¦99953,
\v 28 yelling¦99956 out¦99956, “Men¦99957 of Yisrael¦99958. Help¦99959 us here. This¦99960 is¦99961 the¦99962 man¦99963 who's¦99964 been teaching¦99981 all¦99978 around the world against Yisrael and¦99968 against \add &Mosheh's¦99969\add* law¦99970 and¦99971 against this¦99976 temple. What's more he brought¦99986 non-Jews here into¦99988 the¦99989 temple¦99990 and¦99991 so has¦99993 defiled¦99993 this¦99997 holy¦99995 place¦99996.”
\v 29 (\add They said this\add* because¦99999 they¦99998 had¦100001 previously¦100001 seen¦100001 Trofimus from¦100004 Ephesus in¦100005 the¦100006 city¦100007 with¦100008 Paul¦100019 and had assumed that¦100013 he had brought¦100017 him into¦100014 the¦100015 temple¦100016.)
\p
\v 30 \add ≈So¦100021\add* now the¦100022 whole¦100024 city¦100023 was¦100020 in an uproar, and¦100025 the¦100028 crowds rushed¦100027 in, grabbed¦100031 Paul¦100033, and dragged¦100034 him¦100035 out of the¦100037 temple¦100038 and¦100039 hurriedly closed¦100041 the¦100044 temple gates.
\v 31 They were ready to¦100051 kill¦100051 \add @Paul¦100050\add*, but the¦100054 commander¦100055 of¦100056 the¦100056 \add Roman\add* soldiers had heard that¦100058 all¦100059 Yerushalem¦100062 was¦100060 in an uproar.
\v 32 Taking¦100066 some soldiers¦100067 and¦100068 officers, he immediately¦100064 ran¦100070 down¦100070 to the temple, and¦100074 when the crowd saw¦100075 the¦100076 commander¦100077 and¦100078 the¦100079 soldiers¦100081 they¦100082 stopped¦100082 beating¦100083 Paul¦100085.
\v 33 The¦100089 commander¦100090 came close¦100087 and took¦100091 hold¦100091 of¦100092 \add @Paul¦100092\add* and¦100093 ordered¦100094 that he be¦100095 tied¦100095 up with¦100096 a pair of chains¦100096 while he¦100099 tried to \add ≈find out\add* who¦100100 he¦100102 was¦100102 and¦100103 what¦100104 he'd¦100106 done¦100107 wrong.
\v 34 Various people in¦100115 the¦100116 crowd¦100117 were shouting¦100113 out different \add #things\add*, and¦100122 not¦100119 being¦100121 able¦100121 to¦100124 make any sense of it because¦100127 of¦100127 all the¦100128 commotion¦100129, the commander ordered¦100130 that \add @Paul¦100132\add* be¦100131 taken¦100131 into¦100133 the¦100134 barracks¦100135.
\v 35 When¦100136 they got¦100138 to¦100140 the¦100141 stairs¦100142, the¦100149 soldiers¦100150 had to lift him up above their heads because¦100151 of¦100151 the¦100152 frenzy of¦100154 the¦100154 crowd¦100156,
\v 36 because¦100158 most of¦100161 the¦100161 people¦100162 were¦100157 following¦100157 and shouting¦100164 out¦100164, “Do away¦100166 with him!”
\s1 Paul's defence
\rem /s1 Paul Speaks to the Crowd; Paul Defends Himself
\p
\v 37 As they were able to¦100170 carry him into¦100171 the fortress, Paul¦100175 called down to¦100177 the¦100177 commander¦100178, “Can I say¦100185 something¦100186 to¦100187 you¦100188?”
\p “You¦100193 speak Greek!”, replied the commander.
\v 38 “Aren't¦100194 you¦100196 the¦100198 Egyptian¦100199 who¦100200 stirred up a group of¦100214 assassins¦100215 a while back and¦100206 led¦100207 them out¦100207 into¦100208 the¦100209 wilderness¦100210?”
\p
\v 39 “No, I'm¦100220 a Jew from¦100227 Tarsus¦100227 in Cilica—a¦100235 citizen¦100235 of¦100233 an¦100233 important city¦100234,” said¦100216 Paul¦100219. “But please, let¦100240 me¦100241 speak¦100243 to¦100244 these people¦100246.”
\p
\v 40 The commander nodded, so¦100249 the¦100254 soldiers stood¦100256 Paul¦100255 on¦100257 the¦100258 stairs¦100259 where he gestured¦100262 to¦100265 the¦100265 people¦100267. The crowd quietened down, and¦100270 so \add @Paul¦100277\add* spoke loudly in¦100278 his¦100278 Hebrew¦100280 language¦100281:
\c 22
\nb
\v 1 “Men¦100283, brothers¦100284, and¦100285 fathers¦100286, listen¦100287 to what I have to say for myself.”
\v 2 When¦100296 they heard¦100295 him speaking¦100308 Hebrew¦100299, they¦100304 became even more¦100303 \add ≈quiet¦100305\add* as he continued,
\v 3 “I¦100309 am¦100312 a¦100313 Jew who grew up¦100323 in¦100325 this¦100328 very city¦100327 to study under Gamaliel¦100332. I've been¦100333 raised according¦100335 to¦100335 every part of¦100337 our¦100337 ancestral¦100338 law¦100339, working hard to obey \nd God¦100343\nd* like¦100344 all¦100345 of you¦100347 here today¦100350.
\v 4 I¦100356 used to persecute¦100356 the followers of ‘The Path¦100355’ even to death¦100359—tying¦100360 up both¦100367 men¦100366 and¦100368 women¦100369 and handing¦100362 them over¦100362 to¦100363 the prisons¦100364
\v 5 as¦100370 the¦100372 Chief¦100373 Priest¦100373 and¦100379 all¦100381 the¦100382 council¦100383 of¦100383 elders¦100383 can tell \add you\add*. I took letters¦100387 from them to¦100389 \add &our¦100391\add* \add Jewish\add* brothers¦100393 in Damascus¦100396 when I went¦100398 so that¦100409 I could arrest the ones there¦100402 and¦100399 bring¦100398 them back here¦100402 to¦100405 Yerushalem¦100407 to be¦100410 punished¦100410.
\s1 Paul explains about his conversion
\rem /s1 Paul Tells of His Conversion
\r (Acts 9:1-19; 26:12-18)
\p
\v 6 “But¦100413 one time as I was travelling¦100415 and¦100416 getting close¦100417 to¦100419 Damascus¦100419 by the middle of the day, suddenly¦100424 a bright light¦100432 from¦100426 the¦100427 sky¦100428 began to¦100429 flash¦100429 around me¦100435
\v 7 and¦100438 I¦100437 fell¦100437 to¦100439 the¦100440 ground¦100441. I¦100443 heard¦100443 a¦100444 voice¦100444 asking¦100445 me¦100446, ‘Saul¦100447, Saul¦100448, why¦100449 are¦100451 you¦100451 persecuting¦100451 me¦100450?’
\v 8 ‘Who¦100457 are¦100458 you¦100458, master¦100459?’ I answered¦100454. And¦100462 the voice said¦100460, ‘I¦100465 am¦100466 \nd Yeshua¦100468\nd*—the¦100469 one from¦100470 Nazaret¦100470 that you¦100472 are¦100473 persecuting¦100473.’
\v 9 The¦100474 other¦100488 people with¦100476 me¦100477 saw¦100482 the¦100479 light¦100481 but didn't¦100490 hear¦100491 the¦100487 voice¦100489 that spoke¦100493 to¦100494 me¦100494.
\v 10 Then¦100496 I¦100495 asked¦100495, ‘What¦100497 do I¦100498 need to do¦100498, \nd master¦100499\nd*?’ ‘Get up¦100506 and¦100501 continue into Damascus¦100509,’ the¦100500 \nd master¦100502\nd* said¦100503. ‘When¦100510 you get there¦100510, you'll be¦100512 told¦100512 everything that¦100515 you've been¦100517 assigned to¦100519 do¦100519.’
\v 11 But¦100521 I¦100524 couldn't see anything because¦100526 the¦100527 brightness¦100528 of¦100531 the¦100529 light¦100530 \add had affected my eyes\add*, so my companions had to lead¦100532 me by¦100532 hand¦100532 into¦100539 Damascus¦100540.
\p
\v 12 “In Damascus there was a man¦100544 named Ananias¦100541—a¦100543 \add non-Jew\add* but who was¦100551 respected by¦100552 the¦100554 Jews¦100556 there¦100555 because he strove to obey \add &Mosheh's\add* instructions¦100549 about living¦100555.
\v 13 \add @Ananias\add* came¦100557 to¦100558 where I was and¦100560 standing¦100561 by¦100561 me said¦100562, “Brother¦100565 Saul¦100564, receive¦100566 your sight¦100566!” That very hour¦100570 I was able to see again and look¦100572 at him¦100574
\v 14 and¦100576 he¦100575 said¦100577, ‘The¦100578 \nd god¦100579\nd* of¦100580 our¦100582 ancestors¦100581 has \add ≈decided in advance\add* to¦100586 reveal his¦100589 plans to you, and¦100590 for you to¦100591 see¦100591 the¦100592 sinless one—to¦100595 hear¦100595 him¦100600 speaking
\v 15 because¦100601 he wants you¦100602 to¦100605 inform people¦100607 everywhere¦100606 what¦100609 you've¦100610 seen¦100610 and¦100611 heard¦100612.
\v 16 \add ≈So¦100613\add* what else are¦100616 you¦100616 waiting¦100616 for? Get up¦100617 and¦100619 wash¦100620 away¦100620 your sins¦100622 by getting immersed¦100618 \add in water\add* as you¦100623 request his¦100629 forgiveness.’
\s1 The crowd riots when Paul mentions preaching to non-Jews
\rem /s1 Paul Sent to the Gentiles; Paul's Call to Preach to the Gentiles
\p
\v 17 “Then when I returned¦100633 to¦100634 Yerushalem¦100635 and¦100636 was praying¦100637 in¦100639 the¦100640 temple¦100641, I fell into a¦100645 trance¦100645
\v 18 and¦100646 saw¦100647 \add @the¦100649 master¦100649\add* telling¦100650 me¦100651, ‘Get out of¦100657 Yerushalem¦100658 in a hurry¦100652 because¦100659 they¦100661 won't¦100660 accept¦100661 what you're¦100662 teaching them about¦100665 me¦100666.’
\v 19 ‘\nd Master¦100669\nd*,’ I¦100667 replied¦100668, ‘they¦100670 know¦100671 that¦100672 previously I¦100673 was¦100674 the one imprisoning¦100675 and¦100676 beating¦100677 the people who believed¦100682 in¦100683 you¦100684,
\v 20 and¦100685 when¦100686 Stephen¦100690 was killed¦100709 after teaching about¦100693 you¦100693, it was me standing¦100698 there and cheering on the ones executing him¦100710 as I held their coats for them.’
\v 21 Then¦100711 \add @the¦100712 master¦100712\add* told¦100712 me¦100714, ‘Get going¦100715 \add now\add* because¦100716 I'm¦100717 sending¦100722 you¦100724 away¦100722 to¦100718 the non-Jews.’ ”
\rem /s1 Paul the Roman Citizen; Paul and the Roman Tribune
\p
\v 22 Until¦100729 this¦100730 point in \add &his¦100731\add* message¦100732, the crowd had been listening¦100725 to \add @Paul\add*, but¦100733 now they¦100734 started shouting¦100738, “Do away¦100739 with this man¦100744! He doesn't deserve to¦100750 live¦100750 in this world¦100742!”
\v 23 Then¦100753 they yelled¦100752 and¦100756 threw¦100757 some of their¦100758 clothes¦100759 down¦100757 onto the ground and¦100760 threw¦100762 dust¦100761 up into¦100763 the¦100764 air¦100766.
\rem /s1 Paul Reveals His Roman Citizenship
\v 24 So¦100788 the¦100769 commander¦100770 ordered¦100767 \add @Paul¦100773\add* to¦100771 be¦100771 brought¦100771 inside into¦100774 barracks¦100776 and ordered¦100782 that¦100782 he¦100784 be¦100789 flogged¦100778 and interrogated¦100779 to¦100793 determine what he'd done that would make¦100787 the crowd react like that.
\v 25 But¦100795 as¦100794 they¦100797 strapped¦100802 him down and stretched¦100797 him¦100800 out¦100797 for the flogging, Paul¦100809 asked¦100803 the¦100805 centurion¦100807 standing¦100806 there, “It¦100818 is¦100818 legal for you¦100819 to¦100820 flog¦100820 a¦100813 Roman citizen who hasn't even been tried?”
\p
\v 26 When¦100823 he heard¦100822 that, the¦100824 centurion¦100825 went to¦100832 the¦100832 commander¦100833 and reported¦100835, “How are¦100840 \add %we¦100840\add* going¦100840 to¦100841 handle this, because¦100843 this¦100845 man¦100844 is¦100847 a¦100846 Roman¦100846 citizen?”
\p
\v 27 \add ≈So¦100850\add* the¦100851 commander¦100852 went in to¦100855 where \add @Paul¦100855\add* was¦100857 and asked him, “Tell¦100857 me¦100858 the truth. Are¦100862 you¦100860 a¦100861 Roman¦100861 citizen?”
\p “Yes¦100867 I am¦100865,” said¦100865 \add @Paul\add*.
\p
\v 28 “Yeah, and¦100872 I¦100878 paid a¦100881 lot for mine as well,” mocked the¦100887 commander¦100875.
\p “Actually mine is¦100895 through birth¦100895,” Paul¦100890 responded¦100891.
\p
\v 29 At this point, the¦100903 soldiers who were about to¦100906 interrogate¦100906 \add @Paul\add* left¦100904 the¦100908 room, and¦100910 even the commander¦100909 was now afraid¦100911 after learning¦100912 that¦100913 he'd¦100915 had a¦100914 Roman¦100914 citizen tied¦100921 down to be¦100920 flogged.
\s1 Paul faces the Jewish council
\rem /s1 Paul's trials; Paul before the Council; Paul before the High Council; Before the Sanhedrin
\p
\v 30 The¦100922 next¦100924 day¦100922, the \add Roman\add* commander \add still\add* wanted¦100925 to¦100926 know¦100926 what was behind the¦100927 anger of the¦100934 Jews¦100935, so he had \add @Paul\add* released¦100936 \add from the cell\add*, but brought¦100954 him¦100937 down¦100954 \add to a room\add* where he'd ordered¦100942 the¦100945 chief¦100946 priests¦100946 and¦100947 the¦100950 \add Jewish\add* council¦100951 to¦100943 convene. Paul¦100956 was given a seat
\c 23
\nb
\v 1 and¦100961 looking¦100960 directly at¦100964 the¦100964 council¦100965 members said¦100968, “Men¦100969, brothers¦100970, all¦100972 my life I have¦100975 lived¦100975 before \nd God¦100977\nd* as¦100975 a good¦100974 citizen¦100975 and with a clean conscience¦100973.”
\v 2 But¦100983 Ananias¦100985 the¦100982 chief¦100984 priest¦100984 commanded¦100987 one of his¦100992 companions to¦100988 whack \add @Paul¦100990\add* on the¦100993 mouth¦100994.
\v 3 “\nd God¦101008\nd* will strike¦101004 you,” said¦101003 Paul¦100997, “you¦101005 grand show-off. You've been¦101010 brought here to judge¦101014 me¦101015 as someone who knows the¦101017 law¦101018 and¦101019 yet you¦101021 violate¦101020 the law¦101020 by commanding¦101021 that I be¦101023 hit¦101023 in the face.”
\p
\v 4 But¦101025 the¦101024 others standing¦101026 there said¦101027, “Are¦101032 you¦101032 insulting¦101032 \nd God's¦101031\nd* chief¦101029 priest¦101029?”
\p
\v 5 “Brothers¦101039,” said¦101033 Paul¦101036, “I¦101038 didn't¦101037 realise that¦101040 he's¦101041 the chief¦101042 priest¦101042, because¦101044 the scriptures teach us not¦101050 to speak¦101051 wrongly¦101052 of \add %our¦101049\add* leaders.”\x + \xo 23:5: \xt Exo 22:28.\x*
\p
\v 6 But¦101054 then, knowing¦101053 that¦101057 the¦101058 council¦101071 consisted of¦101062 some from the sect¦101062 of the¦101063 Sadducees¦101062 and some from the¦101065 Pharisees'¦101066 party¦101066, Paul¦101056 continued loudly, “Men¦101072, brothers¦101073, like my father, I'm¦101074 a¦101075 member¦101075 of the Pharisees¦101079 and I'm¦101085 being¦101086 tried¦101086 today concerning¦101080 the¦101081 hope¦101081 and¦101082 the resurrection¦101083 of¦101084 the¦101084 dead¦101084!”
\p
\v 7 Well, as soon as he said¦101092 this¦101087, the council was¦101105 split¦101105 as the Pharisees¦101098 and¦101100 the Sadducees¦101102 started arguing.
\v 8 (Sadducees¦101108 say¦101111 that¦101123 the dead don't \add ≈come back to¦101113 life\add*, and there's no¦101112 \add #messengers¦101117\add* and no \add #spirits¦101119\add*, but¦101121 the¦101120 Pharisees¦101120 believe there is¦101122.)
\v 9 \add ≈So\add* then there was a¦101127 big¦101128 kerfuffle because some¦101131 of¦101133 the¦101133 teachers¦101135 of¦101137 the¦101137 law spoke up¦101130 for the¦101139 Pharisees¦101140 saying¦101144, “We¦101147 don't see¦101147 anything wrong¦101146 with this¦101151 man¦101150. Maybe it was a¦101154 spirit¦101154 or¦101157 one \add of\add* \add &God's¦101158\add* \add #messengers¦101158\add* that spoke¦101155 to¦101156 him¦101156.”
\p
\v 10 But¦101162 the¦101171 \add Roman\add* commander¦101172 was¦101169 afraid¦101169 that they might¦101174 tear¦101174 Paul¦101176 apart¦101174 in the¦101181 big¦101161 argument, so he called for his soldiers to come¦101184 down¦101184 to¦101186 uplift \add @Paul¦101187\add* and¦101193 return him to¦101192 the¦101195 barracks¦101196.
\p
\v 11 The¦101197 following¦101199 night¦101200, the¦101203 \nd master¦101204\nd* appeared to¦101202 \add @Paul¦101202\add* and told¦101205 him, “Be¦101206 brave because¦101209 just like¦101208 you¦101210 testified¦101210 about¦101213 me¦101214 here in Yerushalem¦101216, so¦101217 too you'll¦101218 tell them about me in Rome¦101222.”
\s1 The plan to kill Paul
\rem /s1 The Plan to Kill Paul; The Plot to Kill Paul; The Plot against Paul's Life
\p
\v 12 The next day¦101227, some of the¦101235 Jews¦101237 gathered¦101231 informally and pledged not to¦101244 eat¦101244 or drink¦101246 until¦101247 they'd¦101251 killed¦101251 Paul¦101255.
\v 13 There were¦101256 more¦101258 than¦101258 forty¦101259 of them in¦101263 this¦101263 conspiracy¦101265,
\v 14 and they approached¦101269 the¦101270 chief¦101271 priests¦101271 and¦101272 elders¦101274 and told¦101275 them, “We've¦101277 \add ≈made an oath\add* to¦101280 taste¦101280 nothing¦101279 until¦101283 we've¦101286 killed¦101286 Paul¦101288, otherwise we'll bring a¦101276 curse¦101276 on ourselves¦101278.
\v 15 So¦101290 you¦101292 guys assemble the¦101304 council¦101305 and report¦101300 to¦101301 the¦101301 commander¦101302 so¦101306 that¦101306 he'll¦101309 bring¦101309 him¦101310 down¦101309 to¦101311 you¦101314 all¦101314 as¦101315 if you need more¦101318 information about¦101321 his actions, and¦101324 then we'll¦101323 have an opportunity to¦101333 kill¦101333 before¦101325 he gets here.
\p
\v 16 But¦101340 a nephew of Paul¦101345 had¦101339 heard¦101339 about this ambush¦101348 and¦101351 went¦101352 in¦101352 to¦101353 the¦101354 barracks¦101356 to¦101359 inform Paul¦101359.
\v 17 Paul¦101363 then called¦101360 one¦101364 of¦101365 the¦101365 Roman centurions¦101366 and told¦101368 him to take¦101373 the¦101369 young¦101370 man¦101370 to¦101374 the¦101375 commander¦101376 because¦101378 he¦101377 had¦101377 something¦101381 to¦101380 tell him¦101382.
\v 18 So¦101385 the¦101383 centurion took¦101386 him¦101387 to¦101389 the¦101390 commander¦101391, explaining, “The¦101394 prisoner¦101395 Paul¦101396, called¦101397 me¦101398 and asked¦101399 me to¦101404 bring¦101388 this¦101400 young¦101402 man¦101402 to¦101405 you¦101406 because he has¦101407 something¦101408 to¦101409 report¦101409 to¦101410 you¦101410.”
\p
\v 19 The¦101417 commander¦101418 took¦101411 the young man by the¦101414 hand¦101415 and led him¦101416 into his private office and¦101419 asked¦101424, “What¦101425 do you¦101428 have¦101428 to tell me¦101430?”
\p
\v 20 “The¦101434 Jews¦101435 have decided¦101436 to¦101438 ask¦101438 you¦101439,” he said¦101431, “that¦101440 tomorrow¦101441 you¦101444 bring¦101444 Paul¦101443 down¦101444 to¦101445 the¦101446 council¦101447 as¦101451 if they wanted to¦101458 question him¦101460 further.
\v 21 But don't¦101463 listen to them¦101465 because¦101467 over forty¦101473 of¦101469 their men¦101471 will be¦101466 lying¦101466 in¦101466 wait¦101466 for him¦101468, and they've sworn not to¦101478 eat¦101478 or drink¦101480 until¦101481 they've¦101483 killed¦101483 him¦101484. In fact, they're¦101488 all set¦101489 up already, just waiting¦101490 for¦101490 your consent.”
\p
\v 22 The¦101495 commander¦101498 told the¦101500 young¦101501 man¦101501 not to¦101505 tell¦101505 anyone¦101504 else about the ambush and dismissed¦101499 him.
\s1 Paul is escorted away in the night
\rem /s1 Paul Is Sent to Caesarea; Paul Transferred to Caesarea; Paul Is Sent to Governor Felix; Paul Sent to Felix the Governor
\p
\v 23 Then¦101511 the commander called¦101512 in two¦101515 of¦101516 \add &his¦101516\add* centurions¦101517 and ordered¦101518 them to prepare¦101519 two-hundred soldiers¦101520 to travel¦101523 as¦101524 far¦101524 as¦101524 Caesarea¦101525, supported by seventy¦101528 mounted soldiers and¦101529 two-hundred more with spears¦101530. They were to be ready to leave by 9pm,
\v 24 along with a horse for Paul¦101543 to ride, and¦101538 were to¦101539 get him safely¦101544 to¦101545 Governor¦101548 Felix¦101546.
\v 25 Then the commander wrote¦101565 a¦101568 letter¦101568 ready to take:
\p
\v 26 ⇔“Greetings¦101584 to¦101580 the¦101580 most¦101581 excellent¦101581 Governor¦101582 Felix¦101583 from Claudius¦101577 Lysias¦101578.
\v 27 I'm writing to you about this¦101587 man¦101586 who had¦101591 been¦101591 captured¦101591 by¦101592 the¦101593 Jews¦101594 here and¦101595 they were about to¦101597 kill¦101597 him when I moved¦101596 in with¦101601 my soldiers because he¦101615 is¦101615 a¦101614 Roman¦101614 citizen.
\v 28 I wanted¦101616 to¦101619 know¦101619 what they¦101626 were¦101626 accusing him¦101627 of, so¦101617 I¦101628 took¦101628 him down¦101628 to¦101630 their¦101633 council¦101632 meeting,
\v 29 but I¦101637 discovered¦101637 that it was¦101638 just some detail about their¦101643 \add Jewish\add* law¦101642 and nothing¦101644 that should result in death¦101647 or¦101648 imprisonment¦101649.
\v 30 Then when I learnt that they have¦101653 a¦101656 plot¦101656 to try to ambush and kill him, I¦101668 sent¦101668 him immediately¦101665 to¦101669 you¦101670, and¦101672 I'll order his accusers¦101674 to¦101675 bring their case before¦101680 you¦101681.”
\p
\v 31 So¦101685 the¦101683 soldiers¦101686 carried out their instructions and took¦101691 Paul¦101693 as far as Antipatris¦101700 during the night¦101697.
\v 32 The¦101701 next¦101703 morning the¦101705 foot soldiers returned¦101712 to¦101713 the¦101714 barracks¦101715, leaving¦101707 the mounted soldiers to take him¦101710 on
\v 33 to¦101718 Caesarea¦101720, where they¦101727 presented¦101727 Paul¦101730 to¦101731 the¦101725 governor¦101726 along with the¦101723 letter¦101724.
\v 34 After reading¦101732 the letter the governor asked¦101737 \add @Paul\add* what¦101740 province¦101741 that he's¦101742 from and¦101743 \add ≈found out\add* that¦101745 he¦101745 was¦101745 from¦101746 Cilicia¦101747.
\v 35 ⇔Then he ordered¦101763 \add @Paul¦101773\add* to¦101772 be¦101772 kept under guard¦101772 \add in the prison\add* in¦101766 Herod's¦101771 palace until he¦101754 would¦101752 hear¦101752 from¦101753 him in the¦101757 future once his accusers¦101758 got there.
\c 24
\s1 The Jews accuse Paul before Governor Felix
\rem /s1 The Case against Paul; Paul before Felix at Caesarea; Paul Appears before Felix; The Trial Before Felix
\p
\v 1 Five¦101777 days¦101778 later¦101774, Ananias¦101782 the¦101780 chief¦101781 priest¦101781 arrived¦101779 along with¦101783 some¦101786 elders¦101785 and¦101787 an¦101788 attorney¦101788 named Tertullos¦101789, to¦101793 bring the¦101793 charges against¦101795 Paul¦101797 before Governor¦101794 \add Felix\add*.
\v 2 Once he was¦101798 called¦101798 to speak, Tertullos¦101804 began¦101801 his¦101800 \add ≈accusation¦101802\add* of¦101806 \add @Paul\add*:
\p “We have seen peace¦101807 under your rule and¦101811 the¦101812 helpful reforms¦101812 that this¦101817 nation¦101816 has experienced through¦101818 your¦101820 provision¦101821,
\v 3 most¦101827 excellent¦101827 Felix¦101828, and we¦101826 gratefully welcome¦101826 everything¦101822 you've done and¦101824 everywhere¦101825 you've done it¦101826.
\v 4 But¦101833 in¦101832 order¦101832 not¦101834 to oblige you¦101837 longer than necessary, in¦101846 your¦101846 gentleness¦101847 we request you¦101842 to¦101841 listen¦101841 to our brief¦101844 statement.
\v 5 This¦101852 man¦101851 here has been found¦101848 to be a¦101854 troublemaker, stirring¦101856 up riots among the¦101860 Jews¦101861 throughout the¦101864 inhabited¦101865 world¦101865, and¦101867 as a¦101866 ring-leader of the Nazarite sect¦101871
\v 6 he even¦101873 attempted¦101876 to¦101877 profane¦101877 the¦101874 temple¦101875 where we¦101880 were able to arrest him.
\v 7 ◘
\v 8 When you¦101910 examine¦101912 him yourself¦101911 you'll discover all¦101914 these¦101915 \add >things\add* that¦101917 we're¦101918 accusing¦101919 him¦101920 of.”
\v 9 The¦101925 Jews¦101926 who had come also¦101924 affirmed all the accusations.
\s1 Paul's defence against the accusations
\rem /s1 Paul's Defense before Felix
\p
\v 10 \add ≈So¦101932\add* the¦101938 governor¦101939 nodded¦101936 for¦101941 Paul¦101935 to¦101948 speak¦101940 and he started,
\p “I¦101956 happily defend¦101956 myself¦101955 today knowing¦101950 that¦101949 you have many¦101942 years¦101943 of experience ruling \add in this region\add*.
\v 11 You¦101958 can \add quickly\add* establish that¦101961 it was¦101964 less than twelve¦101968 days¦101966 ago that¦101970 I¦101971 arrived in¦101973 Yerushalem¦101975 to worship¦101972 there¦101964.
\v 12 They¦101982 never found¦101982 me¦101983 in¦101979 the¦101980 temple¦101981 arguing with¦101984 anyone¦101985 or¦101987 stirring up the crowd¦101991, and nor¦101996 was I doing that in¦101993 the¦101994 \add ≈Jewish¦101995 meeting¦101995 halls¦101995\add* or anywhere in¦101997 the¦101998 city¦101999
\v 13 and so they¦102004 can't provide evidence to you to support¦102002 any of their \add ≈accusations¦102011\add*.
\v 14 However, I¦102013 must confess¦102013 that¦102017 I follow ‘The¦102019 Path¦102020’ that¦102021 they¦102022 call¦102022 a¦102023 sect¦102023 which¦102021 serves¦102025 the¦102026 \nd God¦102028\nd* of our ancestors¦102027 and believes¦102029 everything in¦102030 \add &Mosheh's\add* law¦102034 as well as the¦102036 writings of the¦102038 prophets¦102039.
\v 15 We put our trust¦102041 in¦102044 the¦102045 same \nd God¦102046\nd* that¦102047 they themselves¦102049 follow, and we all anticipate a¦102052 resurrection¦102052 of¦102056 those who serve God and¦102058 those who don't.
\v 16 Because of this¦102061, I also¦102062 strive to maintain a¦102067 clean conscience¦102067 in everything¦102077, before \nd God¦102072\nd* and¦102073 the¦102074 people¦102075.
\p
\v 17 After several years¦102079 away, I¦102089 arrived¦102089 \add in Yerushalem\add* bringing offerings¦102091 to help my¦102088 own people and¦102090 before God.
\v 18 That¦102093 was when they¦102095 found¦102095 me¦102096 in¦102098 the¦102099 temple¦102100 after going through the \add ≈purification¦102097\add* ceremony, and without either a¦102103 crowd¦102103 or a commotion¦102106 around me.
\v 19 But¦102108 there were some¦102107 Jews¦102113 from¦102110 \add the province of\add* Asia¦102112 \add Minor\add* who¦102114 are¦102115 the ones who should be¦102119 appearing¦102119 here before¦102117 you¦102118, and¦102120 they should be¦102121 the ones accusing¦102121 me if¦102122 they could find anything¦102123 against¦102125 me¦102126.
\v 20 Even these¦102130 ones here \add now\add* should explain what¦102133 \add &their\add* council¦102142 found¦102134 me¦102139 guilty of¦102139,
\v 21 other than¦102143 my calling¦102149 out¦102149 that¦102155 I¦102159 was¦102160 being¦102160 judged¦102160 because I believe in the¦102157 resurrection¦102157 of¦102158 the¦102158 dead¦102158.
\p
\v 22 But¦102167 Felix¦102171 who was already familiar with followers of ‘The Path¦102179’, announced that the¦102183 trial would be adjourned until Commander¦102184 Lysias¦102182 arrived¦102185 when¦102181 he would¦102186 investigate¦102186 further.
\v 23 He ordered the¦102192 centurion¦102194 to¦102195 keep¦102195 \add @Paul\add* confined, but¦102200 under \add ≈relaxed¦102201\add* conditions and¦102202 \add ^allowing any\add* of¦102205 his¦102205 friends to bring him¦102211 anything he needed.
\s1 Paul speaks to the governor and his wife
\rem /s1 Paul Held in Custody; Paul before Felix and Drusilla
\p
\v 24 However, several days¦102214 later¦102212 Felix¦102219 returned there, \add this time\add* with¦102220 his¦102222 wife¦102224 Drusilla¦102221 who was¦102226 Jewish. He¦102229 sent¦102229 for¦102229 Paul¦102231 and¦102232 listened¦102233 to him¦102234 explain about¦102235 faith¦102241 in¦102237 \nd the¦102238 messiah¦102238\nd*.
\v 25 But¦102243 when the subject turned to righteousness¦102246 and¦102248 self-control¦102249 and¦102251 the¦102252 coming¦102255 judgement¦102253, Felix¦102262 became¦102260 very uncomfortable and¦102269 told him, “That's enough for now. If I have¦102271 time¦102268 in the future, I'll¦102272 send for you¦102273.”
\v 26 However he was also¦102276 hoping¦102277 that¦102278 Paul¦102284 would¦102280 give¦102280 him¦102281 money¦102279, so¦102288 he often sent¦102292 for¦102292 him¦102291 and spoke with¦102295 him¦102295.
\p
\v 27 This went on for two¦102296 years¦102296, until Felix¦102302 was replaced \add as governor\add* by Porcius¦102303 Festus¦102304, but¦102306 wanting¦102305 to¦102309 stay in the¦102310 good books of the¦102312 Jews, Felix¦102313 left¦102314 Paul¦102317 confined¦102318.
\c 25
\s1 Tried yet again, Paul appeals to Caesar
\rem /s1 The Trial Before Festus; Paul Appeals to the Emperor; Paul Appears before Festus
\p
\v 1 After¦102325 Festus¦102319 had¦102321 been in the¦102322 area for three¦102326 days¦102327, he made the uphill trip from¦102331 Caesarea¦102332 to Yerushalem¦102330.
\v 2 There the¦102338 chief¦102340 priests¦102340 and¦102341 the¦102342 Jewish leaders¦102343 repeated their case against¦102346 Paul¦102348, and¦102349 they¦102350 implored¦102350 \add @Festus¦102351\add*,
\v 3 asking¦102352 as a¦102353 \add special\add* favour¦102353, that he¦102358 would order \add @Paul¦102359\add* to be sent¦102358 to¦102361 Yerushalem¦102362 (because they wanted to ambush¦102363 and kill¦102365 him¦102366 on¦102367 the¦102368 way¦102369).
\v 4 But Festus¦102373 replied¦102374 that¦102370 he'd keep¦102375 Paul¦102377 in¦102378 Caesarea¦102380 and¦102383 he was heading back there soon,
\v 5 so¦102389 he¦102394 told¦102394 them, “Some of your leaders can go¦102397 there with¦102397 me, and if¦102398 the¦102403 man¦102404 has done anything¦102399 wrong, they can explain their \add ≈accusations¦102407\add* there.”
\p
\v 6 After staying¦102409 seven or eight¦102417 days¦102413 in Yerushalem, \add @Festus\add* and company travelled back to¦102421 Caesarea¦102422, and the¦102424 next¦102425 day¦102424 he sat¦102426 at the¦102428 judge's bench and ordered¦102430 that Paul¦102432 be¦102434 brought¦102434 in.
\v 7 When¦102436 he arrived¦102435, the¦102440 Jews¦102444 who'd come¦102443 from¦102441 Yerushalem¦102442 stood¦102438 facing \add @Paul¦102439\add* and presented many¦102445 heavy¦102447 charges¦102448 which¦102454 they¦102456 weren't able¦102456 to¦102458 prove.
\v 8 Paul¦102460 defended¦102461 himself, stating that¦102463 \add %he¦102478\add* hadn't sinned¦102478 against¦102465 any Jewish law¦102467 or against¦102471 the¦102472 temple¦102473, nor¦102474 against¦102475 any Roman law.
\p
\v 9 However, Festus¦102480 wanted¦102483 to be able to¦102488 grant¦102488 a favour¦102487 to the¦102484 Jews¦102485, so in response¦102489 to¦102491 Paul¦102491 he¦102492 asked¦102492, “Are¦102493 you¦102493 willing¦102493 to¦102494 be returned¦102496 to Yerushalem¦102495 so that I can judge¦102501 you about¦102498 these¦102499 \add >things\add* there¦102497?”
\p
\v 10 “I'm¦102514 here facing Caesar's¦102512 representative,” Paul¦102507 replied¦102504. “and it's¦102517 quite appropriate¦102517 for me¦102516 to¦102518 be¦102518 judged¦102518 here. I've¦102522 done¦102522 nothing¦102520 wrong¦102522 to¦102519 these Jews¦102519 as¦102523 you¦102525 can no doubt see¦102528 for yourself.
\v 11 If¦102529 I¦102537 had¦102537 done¦102537 anything wrong¦102533, anything¦102538 worthy¦102535 of¦102536 the death¦102536 sentence, then¦102534 I'm¦102540 \add ^ready to accept\add* death¦102542. On¦102544 the¦102544 other¦102544 hand¦102544, if¦102543 none of¦102547 their \add ≈accusations¦102549\add* are¦102549 true¦102546, no¦102551 one¦102551 can hand me¦102552 over to¦102555 them¦102555. I¦102558 appeal¦102558 to¦102557 Caesar¦102557.”
\p
\v 12 So Festus¦102561 conferred with his legal counsel¦102566 and answered¦102567, “Well, you've¦102569 appealed¦102569 to¦102568 Caesar¦102568 so you'll¦102572 go¦102572 to¦102570 Caesar¦102571!”
\s1 King Agrippa wants to hear from Paul
\rem /s1 Festus Consults King Agrippa; Paul before Agrippa and Bernice
\p
\v 13 Several days¦102573 later, King¦102579 Agrippa¦102577 and¦102580 \add his sister\add* Bernice¦102581 arrived¦102582 in Caesarea¦102585 to meet Festus¦102589.
\v 14 As¦102590 they¦102594 \add had planned to\add* stay¦102594 on for a few days¦102593, Festus¦102598 mentioned about¦102603 Paul¦102605 to the¦102599 king¦102600, telling¦102606 him, “There's¦102609 a¦102613 prisoner¦102613 here that Felix¦102612 left¦102610 behind.
\v 15 When I was in¦102618 Yerushalem¦102619, the¦102622 chief¦102623 priests¦102623 and¦102624 Jewish elders¦102626 reported¦102620 to me, wanting a conviction¦102632 of this man.
\v 16 I¦102636 told them that¦102637 it's¦102639 not¦102638 the¦102640 Roman¦102641 way to¦102642 convict someone until they've been able to¦102653 face¦102654 \add &their¦102656\add* accusers¦102657 and¦102660 make \add &their¦102658\add* defence¦102661 against the¦102664 accusations¦102651.
\v 17 So¦102667 as soon as they arrived¦102666 here¦102669, on¦102675 the¦102675 very next¦102676 day¦102675 I sat¦102677 on¦102678 the¦102679 judge's bench and ordered¦102681 the¦102683 man¦102684 to¦102682 be¦102682 brought¦102682 in
\v 18 that they¦102692 were¦102692 accusing, but they didn't¦102690 raise even one¦102690 of¦102694 the \add #charges¦102691\add* that¦102694 I¦102695 expected.
\v 19 Instead they¦102708 raised some¦102703 issues¦102701 about¦102704 their¦102705 own¦102706 beliefs and¦102712 about¦102713 some dead person called \nd Yeshua¦102715\nd* that Paul¦102720 reckoned was¦102718 still living¦102721.
\v 20 I¦102724 was puzzled¦102722 about this debate¦102730 and asked¦102731 him if¦102732 he wanted¦102733 to be judged¦102740 about¦102741 these¦102742 \add >things\add* in Yerushalem¦102738.
\v 21 But¦102744 Paul¦102745 appealed¦102746 to¦102747 be¦102747 kept¦102747 safe from them until he could face the¦102752 emperor¦102753, so I¦102755 ordered¦102755 him¦102757 to¦102756 be¦102756 kept¦102756 \add in prison\add* until¦102758 I¦102760 can send¦102760 him¦102762 to¦102763 Rome.”
\p
\v 22 “I'd¦102771 quite like¦102771 to¦102776 hear¦102776 him myself¦102773,” Agrippa¦102765 said¦102765.
\rem /s1 Paul Before Agrippa; Paul Speaks to Agrippa; Paul Brought before Agrippa
\p “Well, tomorrow¦102779,” replied¦102780 Festus¦102769, “you'll¦102781 be¦102781 able to hear¦102781 from¦102782 him¦102782.”
\p
\v 23 So¦102784 the¦102783 next¦102785 day¦102783, \add King\add* Agrippa¦102788 and¦102789 Bernice¦102791 formally arrived¦102786 with¦102792 all their attendants and¦102795 entered¦102796 the¦102798 auditorium¦102800 to greet the commanders¦102804 and¦102805 the \add ≈prominent¦102809\add* men¦102806 of the¦102811 city¦102812, and¦102813 then Festus¦102816 ordered¦102814 for Paul¦102819 to be¦102817 brought¦102817 in.
\v 24 Festus¦102823 started, “King¦102825 Agrippa¦102824 and¦102826 everyone¦102828 present¦102829, observe¦102832 the¦102838 prisoner who a multitude¦102839 of¦102840 Jews¦102841 pleaded¦102842 with¦102844 me¦102844 about both¦102846 in Yerushalem¦102847 and¦102848 here¦102849, that¦102833 he doesn't deserve to still be alive¦102856.
\v 25 But¦102859 I¦102858 haven't discovered anything that he's done¦102867 that's worthy¦102863 of¦102865 a death¦102865 sentence, and¦102870 since he himself¦102869 appealed¦102874 to¦102875 the¦102875 emperor¦102876, I¦102877 judged¦102877 that that's where he should be¦102878 sent¦102878.
\v 26 The problem is¦102889 that I¦102889 don't¦102888 have¦102889 any¦102884 charge to¦102885 write¦102885 to¦102886 my¦102886 master¦102887 and so¦102890 I've¦102892 brought¦102892 the prisoner out¦102892 \add ≈in front of\add* you so¦102902 that¦102902 after you \add ≈examine¦102904\add* him, I¦102906 might¦102906 have¦102906 something¦102908 I¦102910 can write¦102910,
\v 27 because¦102912 it¦102914 doesn't seem logical to¦102913 me¦102913 to transport a¦102916 prisoner¦102916 \add to Rome\add* if there's no charges¦102922 specified against¦102920 him¦102921.”
\c 26
\s1 Paul's defence before Festus and Agrippa
\rem /s1 Paul Defends Himself before Agrippa
\p
\v 1 \add ≈So¦102925\add* then¦102936 Agrippa¦102924 told¦102929 Paul¦102928, “Okay, you¦102931 can give your side \add now then\add*.”
\m So Paul¦102938 gestured with his¦102941 hand¦102942 and began:
\p
\v 2 “Concerning¦102944 all¦102945 the charges which¦102946 have been¦102947 brought against me by¦102948 the¦102949 Jews¦102949, King¦102950 Agrippa¦102951, I¦102952 consider¦102952 myself¦102953 fortunate
\v 3 because you're¦102966 an expert on Jewish customs¦102976 and¦102978 issues¦102979, so¦102981 I¦102982 ask you¦102982 to¦102985 listen¦102985 to me¦102986 patiently¦102984.
\p
\v 4 “All¦103007 the¦102987 Jews¦103009 know¦103006 about my¦102991 early life, from my upbringing in my own country¦103001 and¦103004 then in¦103003 Yerushalem¦103005.
\v 5 They \add also\add* know¦103010, and could¦103014 even testify¦103015 to you if¦103013 they¦103014 would¦103014 admit it, that¦103016 I followed our¦103022 religious¦103023 laws very closely as a¦103025 member¦103025 of the Pharisees¦103025 party¦103025.
\v 6 ⇔Yet now¦103027 I¦103041 stand¦103041 judged¦103042 for believing in the same hope as our¦103035 ancestors¦103034—that hope promised¦103036 by¦103038 \add &our¦103039\add* \nd God¦103040\nd*
\v 7 who we twelve¦103046 tribes¦103046 diligently serve¦103053 day¦103052 and night¦103050 in¦103048 order to¦103056 obtain—the¦103060 hope¦103060, \add ≈your¦103067 majesty¦103067\add*, for which¦103044 I'm¦103061 being¦103061 indicted¦103061.
\v 8 So why¦103068 do you¦103072 all¦103072 find it unbelievable¦103069 that \nd God¦103075\nd* can raise¦103077 the¦103076 dead¦103076?
\p
\v 9 “I¦103078 too \add originally\add* thought¦103081 it¦103090 necessary¦103090 to¦103093 do¦103093 many¦103091 things to¦103083 oppose the¦103084 cause \nd of¦103087\nd* this \nd Yeshua¦103087\nd* from¦103089 Nazaret¦103089.
\v 10 So in¦103099 Yerushalem¦103100 I worked to have many¦103102 of¦103104 these innocent believers locked¦103109 up¦103109 in¦103107 \add #prison¦103108\add* with the¦103110 authority¦103114 of the¦103112 chief¦103113 priests¦103113, or if they were to be killed¦103116, I¦103119 would add my vote¦103121 against¦103119 them¦103119.
\v 11 And¦103122 in¦103123 all¦103124 the¦103125 \add ≈Jewish¦103126 meeting¦103126 halls¦103126\add*, \add when I found believers\add* I punished¦103128 them¦103129 and forced¦103130 them¦103130 to¦103131 curse \add God\add* and¦103133 \add ≈treated them with extreme anger\add*, even¦103138 travelling to¦103139 further away towns¦103142.
\s1 Paul tells about his commission
\rem /s1 Paul Tells of His Conversion
\rem /r (Acts 9:1-19; 22:6-16)
\p
\v 12 “So¦103152 it was one time travelling¦103146 to¦103147 Damascus¦103149 carrying permission¦103153 letters from the¦103156 chief¦103157 priests¦103157
\v 13 that in the middle¦103159 of the day¦103158, \add ≈your¦103164 majesty¦103164\add*, I¦103163 saw¦103163 a¦103173 bright light¦103173 from the¦103167 sky¦103165—even brighter¦103168 than the¦103169 sun¦103170—and it shone¦103171 on me¦103172 and¦103174 my companions.
\v 14 All¦103179 of¦103183 us¦103183 fell¦103182 down¦103182 to¦103184 the¦103185 ground¦103186 and I¦103187 heard¦103187 a¦103188 voice¦103188 speaking¦103190 to¦103191 me¦103192 in¦103195 Hebrew¦103196 saying¦103190, ‘Saul¦103198, Saul¦103199, why¦103200 are¦103202 you¦103202 persecuting¦103202 me¦103201? Life will be¦103203 difficult¦103203 for¦103204 you if you resist me.’
\p
\v 15 ‘Who¦103211 are you, master¦103213?’, I asked.
\p “And¦103215 the¦103214 \nd master¦103216\nd* responded¦103217, ‘I¦103218 am¦103219 \nd Yeshua¦103220\nd*, the one you're¦103222 persecuting¦103223.
\v 16 Now get up¦103225, because¦103234 I¦103235 let you¦103236 see me in¦103232 order to¦103237 appoint¦103237 you¦103238 as \add &my¦103240\add* servant and¦103241 my witness¦103242—to tell the people what you've¦103245 seen¦103245 and¦103248 what you will¦103249 be¦103249 shown.
\v 17 I will rescue¦103251 you¦103252 from¦103253 \add &your¦103254\add* own people¦103255 and¦103256 from¦103257 the¦103258 non-Jews that I'm¦103262 sending¦103265 you¦103267 out¦103265 to,
\v 18 to¦103268 open¦103268 their¦103270 minds and for¦103271 them to¦103274 turn¦103274 back¦103274 from¦103275 darkness¦103276 to¦103277 light¦103278 and¦103279 from¦103281 Satan's¦103284 power¦103282 to¦103285 \nd God¦103287\nd*, so that¦103288 they \add ≈can be forgiven¦103291\add* for \add &their\add* sins¦103292 and¦103293 receive¦103289 an inheritance among¦103295 the¦103296 ones who've been¦103297 made guiltless by¦103298 \add &their\add* faith¦103298 in¦103300 me¦103301.’
\s1 Paul tells about his ministry
\rem /s1 Paul Tells of His Work; Paul Tells of His Preaching
\p
\v 19 “So then, King¦103303 Agrippa¦103304, I¦103306 couldn't disobey that vision¦103310
\v 20 so I preached first¦103315 to¦103312 those in¦103313 Damascus¦103314 and¦103317 then in¦103319 Yerushalem¦103319 and¦103322 all¦103321 of¦103327 Yudea¦103327, and¦103328 then in \add the towns of\add* the¦103329 non-Jews. I¦103333 preached about turning from their sins and¦103335 turning¦103336 back¦103336 to¦103337 \nd God¦103339\nd*, and then actually living like people who had repented¦103342.
\v 21 It was because of these¦103346 \add >things\add* that the¦103349 Jews¦103349 grabbed me¦103347 in¦103354 the¦103355 temple¦103356 and tried¦103357 to¦103358 do me¦103358 in.
\v 22 So¦103360 it is that¦103362 with \nd God's¦103366\nd* help¦103359 until¦103367 this¦103370 very day¦103369 I¦103371 have¦103371 stood¦103371 testifying¦103372 to¦103374 commoners and¦103376 leaders, teaching¦103380 nothing¦103378 other than what¦103381 Mosheh¦103389 and the¦103383 prophets¦103384 had already written that would happen¦103387—
\v 23 that the¦103392 \nd messiah¦103393\nd* would \add come and\add* suffer¦103391 and as the first¦103395 of the¦103398 dead¦103398 to \add ≈come back to life\add*, he is¦103400 going¦103400 to¦103402 be¦103402 proclaiming¦103402 light¦103399 to both¦103404 Jews and¦103406 non-Jews.”
\rem /s1 Paul Appeals to Agrippa to Believe
\p
\v 24 Then¦103410 speaking about¦103411 \add @Paul's¦103411\add* \add ≈defence¦103412\add*, \add Governor\add* Festus¦103414 said¦103419, “Paul¦103421, you're¦103420 stark, raving¦103420 mad¦103420! All your education has turned¦103429 you¦103424 into¦103427 an idiot.”
\p
\v 25 But¦103431 Paul¦103432 said¦103435, “I¦103434 haven't gone mad¦103434, most¦103436 excellent¦103436 Festus¦103437, but¦103438 I'm¦103443 speaking¦103443 the truth¦103439 in all seriousness.
\v 26 The¦103448 king¦103449 knows what I'm¦103454 talking about and¦103452 that's why I'm speaking boldly¦103453, because¦103456 none of¦103459 these¦103459 \add >things\add* were done¦103468 in¦103466 a¦103467 corner¦103467 and I'm sure that¦103469 none of it has escaped¦103455 his notice¦103455.
\v 27 King¦103471 Agrippa¦103473, do you¦103470 believe¦103470 what the¦103474 prophets¦103475 \add wrote\add*? Yes, I'm¦103476 sure you¦103478 do.”
\p
\v 28 But¦103480 Agrippa¦103481 answered¦103481 Paul¦103484, “Do you think you can talk me¦103488 into becoming a¦103491 follower¦103491 of¦103491 the¦103491 messiah¦103491 in just that short time¦103487?”
\p
\v 29 “I¦103498 hope¦103498 \nd to¦103502 God¦103502\nd*”, said¦103496 Paul¦103496, “whether it takes a¦103505 short time¦103505 or a¦103508 long time¦103508, that both¦103503 you¦103512 and¦103514 the¦103516 others listening¦103517 today¦103519 will become¦103520 just like me except¦103527 for these¦103530 chains¦103529.”
\p
\v 30 Then the¦103537 king¦103538 and¦103539 the¦103540 governor¦103541 both stood¦103535, along with Bernice¦103544 and¦103545 the¦103546 others sitting¦103547 with¦103547 them¦103548
\v 31 and¦103549 as they left they were¦103551 saying¦103551 to¦103552 each other, “That¦103555 man¦103566 hasn't done¦103564 anything deserving of¦103558 death¦103558 or¦103560 imprisonment¦103561.”
\p
\v 32 “Yes, he could¦103574 have¦103573 been \add ≈set free\add*,” replied¦103572 Agrippa¦103568, “if he¦103580 hadn't appealed¦103580 to Caesar¦103581.”
\c 27
\s1 Paul is escorted off overseas
\rem /s1 Paul Sails for Rome; Paul's journey to Rome
\p
\v 1 \add ≈So¦103583\add* when¦103582 it¦103584 was¦103584 considered the right season to¦103586 sail¦103586 to¦103588 Italy¦103590, Paul¦103595 and¦103596 some¦103597 other¦103598 prisoners¦103599 were put under the guard of a¦103600 centurion¦103600 named¦103602 Julius (from the¦103604 Augustan Regiment).
\v 2 We all boarded¦103606 on¦103608 a¦103608 ship¦103608 from Adramyttium that was¦103619 about to¦103612 sail¦103612 to¦103613 various places¦103618 along¦103615 the¦103616 coast of Asia¦103617 \add Minor\add*. Aristarchus¦103623, a¦103625 Macedonian¦103625 man from¦103626 Thessalonica¦103626, was with us as we departed.
\p
\v 3 The¦103627 next¦103629 day¦103629 we¦103630 docked at¦103631 Tsidon¦103632 where Julius \add very\add* kindly allowed¦103640 Paul¦103638 to¦103641 visit some of his¦103642 friends¦103643 so¦103634 they could provide for his needs.
\v 4 When¦103648 we¦103650 left \add again\add*, the¦103651 ship stayed between Cyprus¦103652 and the mainland because¦103653 of¦103653 the¦103655 unfavourable winds¦103656,
\v 5 then¦103660 after sailing¦103668 past Cilicia¦103665 and¦103666 Pamfylia, we¦103669 docked at Myra¦103673 in \add the province\add* of¦103675 Lycia¦103675.
\v 6 There¦103677 the¦103679 centurion¦103680 found¦103678 a¦103681 ship¦103681 from¦103682 Alexandria¦103682 that was sailing¦103683 to¦103684 Italy¦103686, so¦103677 he¦103687 boarded us¦103688 on that.
\p
\v 7 After many¦103693 days¦103695 of slow¦103696 and¦103697 difficult¦103698 sailing¦103696, we came alongside¦103700 Cnidus¦103702 but the¦103706 wind¦103707 wasn't helpful to us¦103705, so we¦103708 sailed¦103708 across to Crete¦103710 and along¦103711 the cape of Salmone¦103712.
\v 8 We sailed¦103715 along¦103715 it¦103716 with¦103713 difficulty¦103713 until we¦103717 came¦103717 to¦103718 a¦103719 place¦103719 called¦103721 ‘Fair Havens’ which¦103724 was¦103728 near¦103725 the¦103727 city¦103727 of Lasea.
\p
\v 9 A lot of time¦103732 had¦103733 now elapsed¦103733 and¦103734 the¦103738 voyage¦103739 was¦103735 starting to become dangerous¦103737 because¦103740 we were already¦103745 heading into¦103746 winter. Paul¦103749 advised¦103747 those¦103747 in charge,
\v 10 telling¦103750 them¦103751, “Men¦103752, I¦103753 perceive¦103753 that¦103754 we're going to¦103775 encounter a lot of damage¦103756 on this voyage¦103777, with¦103755 loss¦103759 not¦103760 only¦103761 of¦103762 the¦103762 cargo¦103765 and¦103766 the¦103767 ship¦103768, but¦103769 also¦103770 our¦103773 lives¦103772.”
\v 11 But¦103779 the¦103778 centurion¦103780 was¦103788 persuaded¦103788 by¦103781 the¦103784 ship¦103785 owner¦103785 and the¦103781 captain¦103782, rather¦103787 than¦103789 by¦103790 what Paul¦103793 had said¦103794.
\v 12 They realised that this harbour¦103798 wasn't suitable to¦103800 winter¦103801 in, so the¦103802 majority suggested leaving there¦103807 with the expectation of being¦103811 able¦103811 to get¦103813 to Crete¦103819 to¦103816 winter¦103816 in the harbour at Phoenix¦103815 (which was open to¦103821 both the¦103822 southwest¦103822 and¦103823 the¦103825 northwest¦103825).
\s1 The severe storm at sea
\rem /s1 The Storm at Sea
\p
\v 13 When¦103828 a¦103829 south¦103829 wind¦103829 started blowing¦103826 gently¦103826, they thought¦103830 they could make it, so they pulled in the anchor and sailed¦103836 along¦103836 close to Crete¦103838.
\v 14 However, a¦103847 short while later¦103839 the wind¦103847 turned to a violent north-easter which¦103849 forced us out to sea.
\v 15 The¦103854 ship¦103855 was¦103852 driven by the wind¦103861 and¦103856 unable to tack to face¦103859 into¦103860 it, so the¦103860 sailors gave¦103862 up¦103862 and just let it be¦103863 driven¦103863.
\v 16 As we passed a¦103866 small island¦103864 called¦103868 Clauda¦103869, we¦103872 got a brief break and were¦103872 able¦103872 to¦103875 resecure the¦103876 lifeboat¦103877
\v 17 and then bring it up¦103879 on board. Then ropes were¦103882 used to¦103880 wrap around the¦103884 ship¦103885 in case it were to run¦103892 aground¦103892 into¦103889 the¦103890 shallows¦103890 at Syrtis¦103891 and the¦103894 gear¦103895 was lowered¦103893,\f + \fr 27:17 \ft It's not certain what was meant here—it might have meant lowering the sails further to catch less wind or even lowering the mast, or else letting out the sea anchor to help keep the back of the ship facing the wind.\f* allowing the wind to drive¦103897 the ship along¦103897.
\v 18 We were¦103901 violently¦103898 tossed¦103901 around by the storm¦103901, and the¦103903 next¦103904 day¦103903 the crew started \add lightening the ship by\add* tipping \add some of the cargo\add* overboard.
\v 19 By the¦103908 third¦103909 day¦103909, the¦103911 sailors themselves \add in desperation\add* were even throwing¦103916 some of¦103913 the¦103913 ship's¦103914 gear overboard.
\v 20 We didn't see either the sun¦103919 or the stars¦103921 for¦103923 several more¦103924 days¦103925—it wasn't a minor storm¦103926 by any means—and by then any remaining hope¦103934 that¦103936 we had \add of staying alive\add* disappeared
\v 21 and¦103940 no one had eaten or drunk much¦103939 at all.
\p Then¦103944 Paul¦103947 stood¦103945 up in¦103948 the middle¦103949 of¦103950 them¦103950 and said¦103951, “Well, you should have¦103956 listened to¦103957 me¦103957 after all and not¦103958 left Crete¦103962, so¦103964 now we have this¦103967 damage¦103966 and¦103968 loss¦103970.
\v 22 Nevertheless I¦103974 think \add %we¦103975\add* can all¦103975 cheer¦103976 up¦103976 because¦103978 not¦103980 a single¦103980 life¦103979 will¦103982 be¦103982 lost—only¦103985 the¦103986 ship¦103987—
\v 23 because¦103989 last night¦103994 one of the \add #messengers¦104005\add* \nd of¦103998\nd* the¦103997 \nd God¦103998\nd* that¦103992 I serve¦104004 came and stood¦103988 beside¦103988 me¦103990
\v 24 and told¦104006 me not¦104007 to be¦104012 afraid¦104008 because I would indeed get to¦104013 stand¦104013 before Caesar¦104010 and¦104014 that \nd God¦104019\nd* has¦104016 granted¦104016 your lives to¦104017 me as well.
\v 25 So¦104025 cheer¦104026 up¦104026, fellows, because¦104029 I'm¦104028 certain that \nd God¦104031\nd* will¦104034 carry out what was told¦104039 to¦104040 me¦104040
\v 26 even though we'll run¦104048 aground¦104048 onto an island¦104042 first.”
\rem /s1 The Shipwreck
\p
\v 27 So it happened that in¦104057 the¦104061 middle¦104061 of¦104062 \add &our\add* fourteenth¦104051 night¦104052 of being¦104055 blown across the¦104058 Adriatic¦104059 Sea¦104059, the¦104065 sailors¦104066 suspected¦104064 that we were¦104068 nearing¦104068 land¦104072.
\v 28 They¦104076 dropped a weighted line and¦104080 measured the ocean depth at around¦104081 40 metres and¦104082 then a bit later it was down to 30 metres.
\v 29 Worrying that the ship might¦104102 run¦104102 aground¦104102 on¦104097 rough¦104099 \add rocks\add*, they threw¦104106 out four¦104108 anchors¦104107 from¦104104 the¦104105 stern¦104105 as they¦104109 hoped¦104109 that daylight¦104110 would come \add quickly\add*.
\v 30 Then¦104113 the¦104114 sailors¦104114 lowered¦104122 the¦104123 lifeboat¦104124 as¦104129 if planning to¦104135 pull out¦104135 some anchors¦104133 from¦104130 the¦104131 bow¦104131, but really planning to desert the¦104119 ship¦104120.
\v 31 However Paul¦104138 told¦104136 the¦104139 centurion¦104140 and¦104141 the¦104142 soldiers¦104143 that if¦104144 the sailors didn't¦104145 remain¦104147 with the¦104149 ship¦104150, everybody couldn't be¦104155 sure to¦104153 be¦104153 saved¦104153,
\v 32 so the¦104158 soldiers¦104159 cut¦104157 the¦104161 ropes¦104162 attaching the¦104163 lifeboat¦104164 and¦104165 allowed¦104166 it to¦104168 run¦104168 aground¦104168.
\p
\v 33 As they waited for the¦104178 daylight, Paul¦104179 encouraged¦104176 them all¦104180 to¦104181 eat something, saying¦104183, “Today¦104185 is \add &our¦104184\add* fourteenth¦104184 day¦104186 and you've¦104189 had¦104191 nothing¦104190 to eat. As we're waiting¦104187,
\v 34 I¦104195 urge¦104195 you¦104196 to¦104197 eat something. This¦104201 will help you to have strength because¦104211 none of you¦104212 are¦104218 going to drown.”
\v 35 Having¦104220 said¦104220 that, he¦104226 gave¦104226 thanks¦104226 \nd to¦104229 God¦104229\nd* \add ≈in¦104230 front¦104230 of¦104230\add* everyone¦104231, and¦104232 breaking¦104233 some bread¦104225, started¦104234 eating¦104235 it.
\v 36 They all¦104240 cheered¦104236 up and also¦104241 started eating.
\v 37 There were¦104248 two¦104260 hundred¦104260 and seventy-six of them on the¦104257 ship¦104258,
\v 38 and¦104265 when they'd all eaten¦104267 what they wanted, they lightened¦104268 the¦104269 ship¦104270 more by throwing¦104271 \add &their¦104272\add* wheat¦104273 into¦104274 the¦104275 sea¦104276.
\s1 The ship runs aground
\rem /s1 The Shipwreck
\p
\v 39 Once it became¦104280 light, they saw¦104289 that¦104294 they were¦104285 close to shore¦104282 in a¦104288 bay¦104286 but didn't¦104283 recognise¦104285 the¦104281 place. After some discussion, they decided to try to save¦104301 the¦104303 ship¦104304
\v 40 \add ≈so¦104305\add* they cut¦104309 away¦104309 the¦104306 anchors¦104307 that were holding them in¦104311 the¦104312 sea¦104313 and untied¦104315 the rudder¦104319 \add handles\add*. Then¦104320 raising the¦104322 foresail¦104323, they let¦104310 the¦104324 wind¦104324 blow¦104325 them into the¦104328 shore¦104329.
\v 41 But¦104331 they hit a¦104333 bank with ocean on both¦104334 sides of it and the¦104336 ship¦104337 ran¦104335 aground¦104335. The¦104339 bow¦104342 of the ship was completely stuck¦104343 but the¦104347 stern¦104349 was¦104350 being¦104350 broken up by¦104352 the¦104353 force¦104354 of the ocean.
\p
\v 42 At this point the¦104357 soldiers¦104359 \add ≈decided\add* that¦104362 they¦104365 should¦104365 kill¦104365 the¦104363 prisoners¦104364 in case any of them decided to swim¦104368 away¦104368 and escape¦104369,
\v 43 but¦104372 the¦104371 centurion¦104373 wanted¦104374 to¦104375 keep Paul¦104377 safe so¦104386 he vetoed that plan. He commanded¦104384 those who could¦104388 swim to¦104390 jump off¦104391 and swim in to¦104398 shore¦104396,
\v 44 and¦104399 for the¦104400 others to grab \add floating\add* planks¦104405 or something off the¦104412 ship¦104413 \add that would float\add*, and¦104414 so¦104415 everyone got¦104416 safely¦104418 onto the¦104420 beach.
\c 28
\s1 On the island of Malta
\rem /s1 In Malta; Ashore on Malta; Paul on the Island of Malta
\p
\v 1 Once everyone was¦104423 safe, we¦104426 discovered¦104426 that¦104427 the¦104429 island¦104430 is¦104431 called¦104431 Malta.
\v 2 And¦104433 the people there showed incredible kindness¦104440 to¦104441 us as they welcomed us and lit¦104442 a¦104445 fire¦104445, as it started raining¦104452 \add again\add* and¦104455 was \add very\add* cold¦104458.
\v 3 However when Paul¦104462 was helping to pick up sticks¦104463 and¦104466 putting¦104467 them¦104467 on¦104471 the¦104472 fire¦104473, a¦104474 viper¦104474 was driven out¦104480 by the¦104477 heat¦104478 and clamped¦104481 its jaws down¦104481 on¦104483 his¦104485 hand¦104484.
\v 4 When¦104486 the¦104489 islanders saw¦104488 the¦104492 snake hanging¦104491 from¦104494 his hand¦104496 they¦104501 decided that¦104507 he must be¦104504 a¦104503 murderer¦104503 who¦104508 had¦104509 survived the shipwreck but \add now\add* fate had \add finally\add* decided his time was¦104515 up.
\v 5 However, \add @Paul¦104518\add* just shook¦104521 the snake off¦104521 into¦104525 the¦104526 fire¦104527 and wasn't harmed¦104530.
\v 6 The locals were¦104533 waiting¦104533 for his arm to swell¦104538 up¦104538 or¦104539 for him to¦104540 fall¦104540 down¦104540 dead¦104542, but¦104545 after a while when¦104548 nothing¦104550 happened they changed¦104555 their¦104555 minds¦104555 and decided that instead, he must be¦104561 a¦104562 god¦104562.
\p
\v 7 The area that¦104569 we had beached in belonged to¦104572 the¦104572 leader¦104573 of¦104574 the¦104574 island¦104575 named¦104576 Publius¦104577. He welcomed us and \add ≈looked after\add* us for three¦104581 days¦104582,
\v 8 but¦104587 while we were there, \add *his\add* father¦104589 got¦104586 sick with¦104596 dysentery¦104594 and \add #fever¦104592\add*. Paul¦104601 went¦104602 in¦104602 and¦104603 placing¦104606 his hands¦104608 on¦104609 him, prayed¦104604 for him and healed¦104610 him¦104611.
\v 9 As a result of that¦104619, anyone on the¦104623 whole island¦104624 who was¦104627 sick came¦104627 and¦104629 was¦104630 cured
\v 10 and¦104632 so the people brought us¦104636 many¦104633 gifts and¦104637 when we left, they¦104639 supplied us with provisions.
\s1 Sailing from Malta to Rome
\rem /s1 Arrival at Rome; From Malta to Rome; Paul Arrives at Rome
\p
\v 11 After¦104647 three¦104649 months¦104650 we¦104652 were¦104652 able to leave on an Alexandrian ship¦104654 that had¦104655 wintered¦104655 at the¦104657 island¦104658. It was decorated with¦104660 carvings of the twin¦104661 ‘gods’.
\v 12 ⇔We¦104666 stayed¦104666 three¦104669 days¦104667 when¦104662 we arrived at¦104664 Syracuse¦104665
\v 13 and then we¦104674 sailed on to Rhegium¦104676. The next day¦104680 a¦104682 south¦104682 wind¦104682 came¦104684 so we sailed on to¦104685 Puteoli¦104686
\v 14 where¦104687 we found¦104688 some believers¦104689 and they encouraged us to¦104694 stay¦104694 for seven¦104697 days¦104696. Then¦104698 we¦104704 \add finally\add* got to¦104701 Rome¦104703.
\v 15 The¦104706 believers¦104707 in \add @Rome\add* had¦104708 heard¦104708 about¦104710 us¦104711, so they came¦104712 to¦104714 meet¦104715 us¦104717 at the Appius¦104720 Market¦104721 and¦104722 the Three¦104723 Taverns¦104724. Paul¦104728 was encouraged to see¦104726 them and thanked¦104729 \nd God¦104731\nd*.
\s1 Paul preaches in Rome
\rem /s1 In Rome
\p
\v 16 When¦104734 we¦104736 got into Rome¦104740 proper, Paul¦104751 was¦104748 allowed¦104748 to¦104753 live by¦104754 himself¦104756, with¦104757 just a soldier¦104761 guarding¦104759 him¦104760.
\rem /s1 Paul Preaches at Rome under Guard; Paul and Jewish Leaders in Rome
\p
\v 17 After¦104764 three¦104766 days¦104765, he called¦104767 together¦104767 the¦104771 leaders¦104775 of¦104773 the¦104773 Jews¦104774 \add in Rome\add*, and¦104777 when they'd all gathered he¦104780 spoke to¦104781 them¦104782, “Men¦104785, brothers¦104786, I¦104784 have¦104790 done¦104790 nothing¦104788 against \add &my¦104791\add* people¦104792 or¦104793 against \add &our\add* ancestral¦104797 customs¦104795. I was taken prisoner¦104798 in Yerushalem¦104800 and handed¦104801 over¦104801 to¦104802 the Romans¦104806
\v 18 who¦104807, after examining¦104808 me¦104810, wanted¦104811 to¦104813 release¦104813 me¦104813 because¦104814 I had done nothing worthy of execution.
\v 19 But¦104823 in defending myself against¦104822 the¦104824 Jews¦104825 there, I¦104826 felt compelled¦104826 to¦104827 appeal¦104827 to¦104828 Caesar¦104828, although I myself had¦104835 no complaint against¦104831 the¦104831 Jews.
\v 20 So¦104841 that¦104840 was why I¦104844 called for you¦104846 and¦104848 wanted to¦104849 address¦104849 you, because¦104851 I¦104859 am¦104859 in \add #chains¦104857\add* for the¦104852 sake of¦104855 Yisrael's¦104855 hope¦104853—\add the messiah\add*.”
\p
\v 21 “We¦104865 haven't received¦104872 any correspondence here about¦104869 you¦104871,” they answered¦104864. “Nor¦104876 have¦104877 any of¦104879 \add &our¦104879\add* brothers¦104880 \add from Yerushalem\add* arrived¦104877 here to report¦104881 any crimes of yours.
\v 22 But¦104889 we¦104888 would \add very much\add* like to¦104893 hear¦104893 from¦104891 you about your thinking¦104895, because¦104898 we've certainly heard about¦104896 this¦104901 new sect¦104900 because everyone is speaking¦104908 out against¦104908 it.”
\rem /s1 Paul Preaches in Rome
\p
\v 23 Then¦104910 they set¦104909 a¦104912 day¦104912 when they¦104913 could visit him¦104916 where he¦104922 was¦104922 staying and so that from morning¦104948 to¦104949 evening¦104950 he could explain¦104922 \nd about¦104930 God's¦104930\nd* kingdom¦104928 and¦104933 show¦104925 them¦104934 how Mosheh's¦104943 law¦104942 and¦104944 the¦104945 prophets'¦104946 writings spoke about \nd Yeshua¦104938\nd*.
\v 24 Afterwards some were¦104955 persuaded¦104955 while others¦104959 didn't find it believable.
\v 25 They argued back and forth, and as they¦104967 were¦104967 about to leave, Paul¦104970 shared this final¦104972 message¦104971, “The¦104977 holy¦104978 \nd spirit¦104976\nd* spoke¦104979 rightly¦104974 through¦104981 Yeshayah¦104982 the¦104983 prophet¦104984 to¦104985 your¦104988 ancestors¦104987, saying:
\q1
\v 26 Go¦104996 to¦104997 these people¦104999 and¦105001 tell¦105002 them,
\q1 When you listen¦105003 you¦105004 will¦105004 hear¦105004 but¦105006 not¦105008 understand¦105009,
\q1 and¦105010 when you look you will¦105012 see¦105012 but¦105014 not¦105016 perceive¦105017 it.
\q1
\v 27 Because¦105020 these people's¦105024 minds have become¦105019 dull¦105019
\q2 and¦105026 they¦105031 can barely hear¦105031 with¦105027 their¦105027 ears¦105028
\q2 and¦105032 they¦105036 shut¦105036 their eyes¦105034,
\q1 just¦105025 in case their¦105035 eyes might¦105038 \add actually\add* see¦105038,
\q2 and¦105041 their¦105042 ears¦105043 might \add actually\add* hear,
